On Mon, 16 Oct 2006, Grunt wrote:
> After acquiring a pet Elvenking recently and being disappointed in its
> abilities - as well as the stories of arch-liches being lackluster pets
> and somewhat unfulfilling as a polymorph form - I was inspired to write -
> and am pround to announce the first version of - the Intelligent Pet Patch.
> This patch incorporates five distinct sub-patches meant to extend the
> abilities of intelligent pets to make them more useful. They are:

   Cool! I'm glad someone finally did it. :)

> 1) Pet weapon/armour handling enhancement.  Intelligent pets can now use
> ranged weapons effectively, and can use any attack against other monsters
> that monsters can use against you (wands, thrown potions, etc.).  To
> complement this, they actively seek out better weapons and armour to use,
> only taking them if they sense that you already have something equal or
> greater in usefulness.  They are now able to use scrolls of remove curse -
> no more cursed thoroughly corroded -3 daggers in the hands of your pet.

   Good idea. Can they use all scrolls, or just that one special case?
If you want to keep extending this patch, you might like to know that
both L and myself (L and I? :) have patches to let monsters wear rings.
L's is better than mine right now, although I am planning to revisit
"monring343" some day.

> 2) Self-polymorphed monster spell casting - you can cast monster spells
> while polymorphed.  Undirected monster spells make use of #monster, and
> directed spells are activated in normal combat.  (This was implemented as
> it was very easy to do so after implementing monsters casting spells at
> each other.)

   This seems overpowered to me; I'd rather see it as an option or a
separate patch. I think tame monsters don't cast spells on purpose,
as a balancing factor; and the player shouldn't be able to cast new
spells when polymorphed, because his brain doesn't know how. (I'm
postulating that you don't need any special knowledge to lay eggs
or fly, I guess, but... yeah.)

> 3) Pet monster summoning, which has been implemented separately and
> disabled by default to to the author's perception that it is extremely
> unbalancing.  With this enabled, pets can summon tame nasties as a
> monster spell (and so can you, if the patch to allow you to cast
> monster spells is enabled).  It is recommended that you do not enable
> this (except for interest's sake)!

   This sounds cool, except that really it should extend the concept
of "tameness" so that each monster is either "peaceful" or "tamed by 
_______", where _______ can be the player or another monster (or the
priest of Moloch, for hostile monsters?). Then, when your pet arch-lich
goes feral, it'll take your army with it. :)

> 4) Pet satiation.  Pets are intelligent enough not to eat if they've eaten
> enough sufficiently recently, but can choke if you feed them too much.
> Intelligent pets will refuse to eat if they can choke, and will keep food
> rations with them if you have food yourself.

   I couldn't believe pets could choke on their food, so I sourcedived,
and I still don't believe it. I think the only way a pet can die from
eating is if it gains a level and turns into a genocided species. Can
you point to the place in the source where pets choke on anything
except leashes?

> 5) Monster aggression towards pets - since your intelligent pets are so
> much more powerful, it is in the interests of monsters to take them into
> account when selecting a target, and so they can now!  This includes all
> of the abilities your pets can use against them, so they will take ranged
> weapon fire in addition to close-combat damage.

   This is excellent.

